we've seen a number of high-profile  criminal cases lately and sometimes  something goes wrong and a mistrial is  declared and unfortunately  people often misunderstand what a  mistrial is now  people know that that means that the  trial has to end  but people think that that means that  the defendant just goes off scot-free  that's not true  a mistrial would be more accurately  described as a mulligan if a mistrial is  declared then the prosecutor can just  simply bring the charges again sometimes  they do sometimes they don't  this happens if there is an error that  is so prejudicial that it cannot be  overcome through  instructions or sometimes when the jury  is deliberating for so long but they  can't come to a decision  they hang and therefore a mistrial is  declared so that the prosecutor can  start the trial again with a new jury  pool so mistrial doesn't mean the  defendant goes free it just means the  prosecutor can bring the charges again  so now you know next time i see you in  court
 

